{
  "dom_id": "step-q344652",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14095797/assets/0ff48bba31ebfe609c1786e3bdcd540aba28b125e67daaac17ff2aed3301749c.png)\n\nA robotics system is expanding a square inspection window by increasing the lengths of its diagonals at a rate of $2.4\\text{\u00a0mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the window increasing when each diagonal measures $10\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=2.4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=10.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2.4\\cdot 10=24{\\text{\u00a0mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square inspection window is increasing at a rate of \n$24{\\text{\u00a0mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$",
